<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-legendre-transform" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Legendre Transform and Dual Coordinates</title>

  <p>
    Consider a <em>strictly convex</em>, <em>differentiable function</em> function
    <m>f: U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}</m>  .
  </p>


  <definition xml:id="def-conjugate-variable">
    <title>Conjugate Variable</title>
    <idx>Conjugate Variable</idx>
    <idx><sortkey>variable</sortkey>Conjugate variable</idx>

    <statement>
     <p>
       The <term>conjugate variable</term> of <m>f(\mathbf{x})</m> is the defined as
     </p>
     <md>
       <mrow>
         \mathbf{p} \amp \triangleq \nabla f(\mathbf{x}) \, .
       </mrow>
     </md>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-legendre-transform">
    <title>Legendre Transform</title>
    <idx>Legendre Transform</idx>
    <idx><sortkey>transform</sortkey>Legendre transform</idx>

    <statement>
      <p>
      The <term>Legendre transform</term> of <m>f</m> is the function <m>f^*: U^* \to \mathbb{R}</m> defined by:
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          f^*(\mathbf{p}) \triangleq \sup_{\mathbf{x} \in U} \left\{ \langle \mathbf{p}, \mathbf{x} \rangle - f(\mathbf{x}) \right\} \, .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </statement>

    <remark>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For an invertible mapping <m>\mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x})</m>, the transformed function may be
          written as:
        </p>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            f^*(\mathbf{p}) = \langle \mathbf{p}, \mathbf{x}(\mathbf{p}) \rangle - f\big(\mathbf{x}(\mathbf{p})\big) \, .
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </statement>
    </remark>

  </definition>

  <context xml:id="context-legendre-transform-application-to-variational-mechanics">
    <title>Application to Variational Mechanics</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="gls-variational-mechanics" text="custom">variational mechanics</xref>, the Legendre transform
        converts the <xref ref="def-euler-lagrange" text="custom">Lagrangian formulation</xref> with coordinates
        <m>(q, \dot{q}, t)</m> into the Hamiltonian formulation with canonical phase-space coordinates <m>(q, p, t)</m>:
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>p_i \triangleq \frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{q}_i}</mrow>
        <mrow>H(q, p, t) \triangleq \sum_i{p_i \dot{q}_i} - L(q, \dot{q}, t)</mrow> \, .
      </md>
    </statement>
    <remark>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Here, a second-order system of <m>n</m> Euler-Lagrange differential equations are mapped to a first-order
          system of <m>2n</m> Hamilton's canonical equations.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </remark>
  </context>
 </subsection>